		<IComment type="e">
			<_id type="a">GUID e8735ed2-4dd1-4b2e-b860-fe00d882e7a1</_id>
			<_myState type="a">8192</_myState>
			<_name type="a">Description</_name>
			<_modifiedTimeWeak type="a">1.2.1990::0:0:0</_modifiedTimeWeak>
			<_description type="p"><IDescription type="e">
				<_textRTF type="a">{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Aptos;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}%0D
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\li720\f0\fs22 The TPS65224-Q1 PMIC Complex Device Driver (\ulwave CDD\ulnone ) provides a comprehensive software interface for \par%0D
controlling and monitoring Texas Instruments' automotive power management solution. This driver enables\par%0D
watchdog supervision, analog-to-digital conversion, Error signal monitoring, Interrupt handling, Voltage Monitoring for BUCKs &amp; LDOs and safety monitoring features.\par%0D
\b\par%0D
\b0 This driver uses i2c driver with optional CRC validation, ensuring reliable configuration and control of \par%0D
the PMIC device. It manages multiple power domains through integrated buck converters and LDO regulators\par%0D
while providing sophisticated error detection and handling mechanisms (BUCK and LDO voltages are \par%0D
\ulwave preconfigured\ulnone  in OTP). The architecture follows AUTOSAR standards, featuring a modular design with clear \par%0D
separation of initialization, \ulwave runtime\ulnone  operations, and interrupt handling.\b\par%0D
\b0 Key features include configurable watchdog timing with window and \ulwave timeout\ulnone  monitoring, 12-bit ADC\par%0D
conversion capabilities, GPIO control, Spread Spectrum, watchdog in trigger mode and an Error Signal Monitor (ESM) for enhanced system safety. \par%0D
The driver maintains a state machine approach for organized execution flow and implements a queue-based \par%0D
transaction handling system for efficient I2C communications. This implementation is specifically designed \par%0D
\pard to meet automotive requirements in focusing on reliability, safety, and deterministic behavior.\par%0D
\f1\fs20\par%0D
}%0D
</_textRTF>
			</IDescription>
			</_description>
			<_modifiedTime type="a">2.18.2026::6:46:21</_modifiedTime>
		</IComment>
